# IPC Fallback Plan

## Goal
Provide an optional developer-side IPC channel for iframe-embedded apps that cannot rely on the standard proxied WebSocket transport, while keeping the frontend thin and backend-driven.

## Transport
- Stand up a lightweight auxiliary service (HTTP/JSON or ZeroMQ) on a dedicated port/Unix socket.
- Keep the schema minimal: e.g. `{action, target, payload}` for push commands, plus a simple “state fetch” endpoint if needed.

## Usage Pattern
1. Default path stays on the existing proxy/WebSocket stack.
2. When an integration can’t stabilize over the proxy, the developer wires the iframe backend to the auxiliary IPC (either listening for commands or polling).
3. The parent framework publishes commands or state deltas over this channel; the iframe applies them locally and acknowledges success.

## Guidelines
- Keep payloads JSON and small; no binary streaming.
- Require an auth token shared with the main app.
- Document the command schema so embedded apps can opt in quickly.
- Treat the IPC as a developer fallback; no automatic runtime switchover.

## Next Steps
- Define the minimal command set (`load_resource`, `set_state`, `push_delta`, etc.).
- Prototype a tiny asyncio-powered HTTP service exposing `POST /ipc/command`.
- Update integration docs so embedded apps know how to subscribe.
